layout handle function
move : In Magento 2, the <move> instruction within a layout XML file is the primary mechanism to change the position of a block or a container element. This instruction sets the declared element as a child of another destination element, optionally specifying its order.
syntax : <move element="[element_name]" destination="[destination_name]" [before|after]="[reference_element_name|true|false]"/>
element: The name of the block or container you want to move.
destination: The name of the new parent block or container where the element will be moved.
before / after (optional): These attributes control the sorting of the moved element within the destination parent.
Set to the name of a sibling element to place it before or after that specific element.
Set to true to place it at the beginning of the destination container.
Set to false to place it at the end of the destination container. 

Example
To move the page.main.title block into the product.info.main container and position it before the product.info.price block, you would use the following XML:
xml
<move element="page.main.title" destination="product.info.main" before="product.info.price"/>
in this example page.main.title is element to move  product.info.main is parent to move this element product.info.price is element to move page.main.title element before product.info.price

container
In Magento 2, a container is a structural element in a layout XML file that acts as a wrapper to organize block elements or other container elements, defining the page structure. Containers themselves do not render output, but they use attributes to control the HTML wrapper and behavior of their child elements. 

Key Functions and Attributes of Containers
htmlTag: Specifies the HTML tag to use for the container (e.g., div, header, footer).
htmlClass: Defines the CSS class for the HTML tag, which affects the styling and positioning on the storefront.
htmlId: Sets the HTML ID for the container, allowing for specific CSS or JavaScript targeting.
after / before: Positions the container relative to another element using that element's name.
remove: A boolean attribute (e.g., true) to remove a container and all its children from the layout.
move: Allows moving a container to a different parent container within the layout structure. 

reference container
In Magento 2, the <referenceContainer/> instruction is used within a layout handle XML file to target an existing container and modify its contents, such as adding or moving child blocks and containers. This affects the storefront by changing the position and structure of elements on the page.

Function Reference: <referenceContainer/>
The primary purpose of <referenceContainer/> is to interact with a container that has already been defined elsewhere (e.g., in a base layout file like default.xml). You use its unique name attribute to reference it. 

 
Key attributes used with <referenceContainer/> include:
name: (Required) The unique identifier of the existing container you want to target.
before / after: Used to position elements added inside the  nced container relative to other elements within that same container.
remove: Set to true to remove the entire referenced container and its children from the page.
display: Set to false to hide the container and its contents without removing them from the layout structure.

reference block
In Magento 2, <referenceBlock> is a layout XML instruction used to modify, move, or remove existing blocks by their name attribute without overriding the entire layout. It acts on specific handles (pages) to update content, such as removing the SKU on product pages, moving elements, or changing templates, directly affecting the storefront
 <!-- Reference the block by its name and set remove to true -->
        <referenceBlock name="product.info.sku" remove="true"/>

Common <referenceBlock> Actions & Storefront Affect:
Remove Block: <referenceBlock name="block.name" remove="true"/> – Removes the block entirely from the frontend.
Disable Block: <referenceBlock name="block.name" ifconfig="path/to/config" /> or adding display="false" – Hides the block but keeps it loaded in memory.
Move Block: <move element="name" destination="new.destination" /> – Re-positions a block within the page structure.
Modify Block: <referenceBlock name="product.info.sku" template="Vendor_Module::new_template.phtml" /> – Changes the .phtml template file.

remove
In Magento 2, you do not directly "remove a layout handle function". Instead, you remove specific blocks or containers associated with that handle using layout XML instructions or programmaticallymmatically via observers/plugins. The primary method is using the <remove> XML tag.

Using Layout XML (<remove>)
This is the standard and recommended way to remove elements in Magento 2. You create a custom layout file in your module or theme to override the original layout and apply the removal instruction. 

Example: Removing the "breadcrumbs" block from the category page. 
Identify the target layout file and block name. The breadcrumbs block often has the name breadcrumbs.
Create an overriding layout file. For the category page, you'd modify the catalog_category_view.xml in your custom theme or module: app/code/Vendor/Module/view/frontend/layout/catalog_category_view.xml.
Add the <referenceBlock name="block_name_to_remove"> with the remove="true" attribute. 

before
In Magento 2, the before attribute in a layout XML file is a powerful tool used to precisely control the order in which blocks or containers are displayed on the storefront. It specifies that the element it's applied to should be rendered before a target element with a specific name. 

Usage in Layout XML
The before attribute is used within <block> or <container> tags in your custom layout XML files (e.g., catalog_product_view.xml in your module's view/frontend/layout directory). 

  <!-- This new_block will be rendered BEFORE the target_block -->
        <referenceContainer name="content">
            <block class="Magento\Framework\View\Element\Template" 
                   name="new_block" 
                   template="Vendor_Module::new_template.phtml" 
                   before="target_block"/>
        </referenceContainer>

name="new_block": This is the unique name of the block you are adding/moving.
before="target_block": This is the critical attribute. It instructs Magento to place the new_block immediately preceding the target_block in the HTML output. The target_block must be an existing block or container name defined in the page's overall layout structure.

after
In Magento 2 layout XML, the after attribute is used within <block>, <container>, or <move> elements to specify that the element should be rendered immediately after a named sibling element within the same parent. This controls the visual order and positioning of elements on the storefront.

The <move> instruction is used to change the position of an element that is already defined elsewhere (e.g., in a parent theme or another module's layout file). 
xml
<move element="product.info.details" destination="product.info.main" after="product.info.name" />
This example moves the product.info.details block into the product.info.main container, and positions it after the product.info.name block. 

